WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.649 --> 00:00:02.066
- Good afternoon.

00:00:03.879 --> 00:00:05.453
- Good afternoon.
- Good afternoon.

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:10.560
- Good afternoon and welcome
to the Chubb Fellowship lecture

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.770
with our distinguished
guest, Faith Ringgold,

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:18.690
award-winning artist, author,
educator and activist.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.990
Today's program is generously
supported by many units

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.460
across Yale University's campus.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:27.120
We wish to thank African-American Studies,

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.690
American Studies Ethnicity,
Race and Migration,

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.090
History of Art,

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.620
Women and Gender and Sexuality Studies,

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.040
the Afro-American Cultural Center,

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.450
Center for the Study of Race, Indigeneity

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.380
and Transnational Migration,
the School of Art.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:44.570
And we especially wish

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:47.500
to thank the Yale University Art Gallery

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.830
for hosting today's lecture
in this beautiful auditorium.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:52.900
So thank you YUAG.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:56.290
Before we begin I ask that
you turn off all cell phones

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:59.500
and refrain from taking any
photographs during the program

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.050
so that you do not
disrupt today's program.

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:05.550
And once we get started the
lights will be fully dimmed

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.670
so just be careful of
your surroundings when you

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.323
because the lights will be off.

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:14.180
My name by the way is Mary Lui,

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:16.440
I'm a professor in the
Department of History

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.740
and the program in American Studies.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.630
I'm also the current head of college

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:23.260
for Timothy Dwight College.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:26.100
As the head of Timothy Dwight
College I have the honor

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.730
of serving as the custodian

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:30.640
of the illustrious Chubb
Fellowship established

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.070
in 1941 out of a prior large donation

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:37.600
for education purposes made in 1936

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.243
by Hendon Chubb a graduate
of the Yale class of 1895.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:46.754
Since its establishment,
the Chubb fund has adhered

00:01:46.754 --> 00:01:49.040
to the goals of providing
encouragement and aid

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.110
to students interested in government

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:53.660
and American public affairs.

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:56.140
The fellowship initially aimed to foster

00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:00.230
among Yale undergraduates an
interest in public service

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:02.890
in local and state affairs.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.340
It grew over the years

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.680
to include numerous distinguished visitors

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.640
in national and international affairs

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:13.650
as well as leaders in the world
of the arts and humanities.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:17.020
Since the 1940s the Chubb
Fellowship Lecture Series

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:20.380
has inspired generations
of Yale undergraduates

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.940
to undertake public service
and pursue leadership roles

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:27.290
in the hopes of creating
a better world today

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.120
and for posterity.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.360
The fellowship has hosted
four U.S. presidents,

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:37.360
George HW. Bush, Gerald Ford,
Jimmy Carter and Harry Truman.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.150
In recent years we've
welcomed a wide range

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:43.810
of national and international
leaders including

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:46.550
environmental activist Bill McKibben

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.690
Ambassador Samantha Power,

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:51.660
physician and humanitarian Dr. Hawa Abdi,

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.140
renowned actor Shah Rukh Khan,

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:58.380
and award-winning songwriter
and musician, Paul Simon.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.570
With this quick description of the history

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.550
and aims of the Chubb Fellowship,

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.510
I'm delighted that we are welcoming

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:08.780
to Yale today the distinguished
artist Faith Ringgold

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.183
as our spring 2018 Chubb Fellow.

00:03:13.228 --> 00:03:16.478
(audience applauding)

00:03:21.622 --> 00:03:23.810
Joining Faith Ringgold today are members

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:25.570
of her family and staff.

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:28.320
Her daughter Michele Wallace is a scholar

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.200
of African American film and
cultural studies (laughing)

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:36.008
(audience applauding)

00:03:36.008 --> 00:03:39.190
and she recently retired from
City University of New York

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:40.820
after a lovely career.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.910
Grace Mathews is Faith
Ringgold's assistant,

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:45.040
there she is.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:46.640
(audience applauding)

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.380
Kyle Mathews and Martha Whitehead are part

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:52.360
of Faith Ringgold's
Anyone Can Fly Foundation

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:54.903
and true core volunteers.

00:03:55.926 --> 00:03:59.860
(audience applauding)

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:03.430
We're delighted that you were
all able to join us today.

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:07.667
Born and raised in depression-era
Harlem, New York City,

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:13.080
Faith Ringgold's career
as an artist, educator,

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.600
writer and activist spans
more than half a century

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.950
and as you can see 60 years.

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:22.240
She earned a bachelor's
degree from City College

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:25.480
of the City University
of New York in 1955.

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.220
She then taught art in New
York City's public schools

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:30.870
and worked on a master's
degree at City College

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.907
which she completed in 1959.

00:04:33.907 --> 00:04:36.200
In the 1960s as an art teacher

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.310
in the New York City Public Schools,

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:41.600
she began a series of paintings
called American People

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.490
that portrayed the civil rights movement

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:45.660
from a feminist perspective.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:49.590
The painting showed ordinary
people, both black and white,

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:52.080
confronting social
barriers while attempting

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.450
to forge relationships
in their everyday lives.

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:57.410
She also organized and fought for works

00:04:57.410 --> 00:04:59.490
of African-American and women artists

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:02.330
to be included in museums and galleries.

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:04.210
She demonstrated against the exclusion

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:05.820
of black and female artists

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:08.470
by New York's Whitney
Museum of American Art

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:12.430
and the Museum of Modern
Art from 1968 to 1970.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:16.720
In 1971 Faith Ringgold
co-founded Where We At,

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.460
a black women artists group.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:22.130
In the 1980s she embarked on projects

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:24.980
that employed the medium
of the story quilt rooted

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:27.150
in African-American communal traditions

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:29.200
of quilting and storytelling

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.940
that have been critical for
connecting families stories

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:34.770
and lives across many generations.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:38.210
Works such as the 1988 Tar Beach Part I

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:40.910
from the Woman on Bridge
Series are currently

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:45.160
in the Solomon R. Guggenheim
Museums permanent collection

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:48.030
and they make up some of
her best known masterpieces

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:49.700
from this period.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:51.310
Faith Ringgold has also produced

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:53.340
numerous public art projects included,

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:56.160
Flying Home Harlem Heroes and Heroines

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.130
for the Metropolitan Transit Authorities,

00:05:58.130 --> 00:06:00.860
a 125th Street subway stop,

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.480
for the Woman's House,

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:05.740
for the Rose M. Singer
Center on Rikers Island,

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:08.625
the Crown Heights Children's History Quill

00:06:08.625 --> 00:06:10.490
at Public School 22 in Brooklyn, New York

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:13.410
along with many others
around the New York City

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:15.720
as well as around the country.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.240
Since the 1990s she is also written

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:20.880
and illustrated many
acclaimed children's books

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.770
such as Tar Beach that was
the Caldecott Honor Book

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:26.610
and winner of the
Coretta Scott King Award.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:28.370
She has helped young readers explore

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:29.690
African American history

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:33.490
through Aunt Harriet's
Underground Railroad In the Sky,

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:35.700
one of my children's favorite books,

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:37.610
Dinner at Aunt Connie's House,

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:40.380
honors civil rights
activist Fannie Lou Hamer,

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:43.130
Mary McLeod Bethune, Zora Neale Hurston

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:45.580
and many more famous black women.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:48.700
If a Bus Could Talk, the
story of Miss Rosa Parks,

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:52.000
won the NAACPs Image Award in 2000.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.220
And for adults she has published
her memoir in 1995 titled,

00:06:56.220 --> 00:07:00.370
We Flew Over the Bridge: The
Memoirs of Faith Ringgold.

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:01.780
She's professor emeritus

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:04.530
at the University of California San Diego

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:09.080
where she taught art from 1987 to 2002.

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:12.100
She is the recipient
of more than 75 awards,

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:15.710
including 22 honorary
Doctor of Fine Arts degrees.

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:17.510
She has received fellowships and grants

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:19.620
from the National Endowment for the Arts,

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:22.750
John Simon Guggenheim Memorial Foundation,

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:25.160
New York Foundation for
the Arts and many more

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:27.260
for painting and sculpture.

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:30.140
And I'm now delighted that she
can add the Chubb Fellowship

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:33.550
to her long list of illustrious awards.

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:35.020
I'm also pleased to say

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:37.670
that the Hopper Windows
Commission Committee

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:40.110
that is the Hopper the
Windows Commissioned Committee

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:43.150
for Hopper College here on Yale's campus

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:45.150
and several members of
which are with us today

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:47.840
including head of college Julia Adams,

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:51.100
has recommended that Faith
Ringgold be the artist

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:54.433
to design new windows for
the Hopper dining hall.

00:07:55.432 --> 00:07:58.599
(audience applauding)

00:08:04.858 --> 00:08:08.530
And again head of college
Julie Adams is here.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:10.808
So this is very exciting news.

00:08:10.808 --> 00:08:14.844
(audience applauding)

00:08:14.844 --> 00:08:18.150
And you are very much
the first public audience

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:19.300
to hear this announcement.

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:22.150
It's been circulating sort of
privately on email this week

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:25.230
so it's very exciting to
hear the announcement.

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:27.510
We're looking forward to
developing a conversation

00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:29.440
with Faith over the coming months.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:33.280
So Faith, that chapter will be continued.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:36.860
But for today I'm delighted
to celebrate her life's work

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:39.770
and her many achievements
and hear her talk,

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:41.730
Anyone Can Fly.

00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:45.852
So please join me in welcoming
Faith Ringgold to the podium.

00:08:45.852 --> 00:08:49.019
(audience applauding)

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:05.653
- Very much.

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:10.023
Good evening.

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:16.450
Thank you so much for inviting
me and for you coming.

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:21.003
This is a great honor.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:23.743
I'm so happy to be here.

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:28.300
This is one of my favorite places.

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:31.450
Back in the day when
I was going to college

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:35.685
if you went to Yale you were hot stuff.

00:09:35.685 --> 00:09:37.180
(audience laughing)

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:39.720
Especially in art.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:42.793
Okay so thank you I'm here.

00:09:44.761 --> 00:09:45.624
All right.

00:09:45.624 --> 00:09:48.791
(audience applauding)

00:09:53.069 --> 00:09:57.652
Okay, I'm going to, in 1948
I made my first work of art

00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:07.030
that I considered complete
and the finished work of art.

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:09.343
and

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:14.630
is that not?

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:16.290
Am I not doing it correctly.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:18.490
Oh wait a minute maybe that's the way.

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:20.610
No nothing's moving.

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:22.053
What am I doing wrong?

00:10:24.910 --> 00:10:26.433
There you go back.

00:10:27.286 --> 00:10:28.213
I want to go back.

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:30.233
Go back.

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:36.343
Just below oh oh.

00:10:39.347 --> 00:10:40.840
There you go.

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:44.210
Yes thank you know they taught me

00:10:45.554 --> 00:10:50.530
at the City College of New York I was told

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:52.360
when I was a young kid

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:54.822
that when I graduated from high school

00:10:54.822 --> 00:10:56.600
I was going to college.

00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:58.710
But I didn't know which college.

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:00.460
So we walked down the street,

00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:03.689
used to see these boys
rushing out of the subway

00:11:03.689 --> 00:11:08.350
at 145th Street and in
Tacoma Avenue in Harlem

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:12.300
and going up the hill to Convent Avenue

00:11:15.447 --> 00:11:19.800
and down the street to the
City College of New York.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:21.820
And I said, Mom why can't I go to that

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:23.230
where these boys going.

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:25.330
She said, "They're going to City College."

00:11:26.507 --> 00:11:27.700
And I said, well why can't I go there?

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:29.710
She said, "Well you can."

00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:32.243
However I didn't know
it was a boy's school.

00:11:33.285 --> 00:11:34.310
(audience laughing)

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:39.310
And so when I went when I
got to be eighteen in 1948

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:43.020
and went down to register

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:45.120
at the City College of New York

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:48.083
that's when I found out
that I couldn't go there.

00:11:49.566 --> 00:11:51.200
And I said what were you talking about?

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:54.627
They said, "No, what do you want be?"

00:11:54.627 --> 00:11:56.360
And I said, be?

00:11:56.360 --> 00:11:58.350
You know women didn't work in those days.

00:11:58.350 --> 00:11:59.370
Be?

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:01.630
I want to do all right.

00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:03.887
They said, "Well wait no.

00:12:03.887 --> 00:12:06.173
"You can't get a liberal arts degree here

00:12:06.173 --> 00:12:08.350
"because this is a boy's school."

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:10.840
So I just refused to hear that

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:15.010
because all my life I had
thought of doing that.

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:18.230
However somebody at the college said,

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:20.490
look she can do it.

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:25.490
She can get a Bachelor
of Arts in art education

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:32.760
and she can major in art
and minor in education

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:38.023
and do her art and teach.

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:40.640
And in a way that was great

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:45.410
because my family had a
lot of teachers in it.

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:49.092
They weren't too cool on the art.

00:12:49.092 --> 00:12:50.180
(audience laughing)

00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:53.400
Now the art was a little, you know, iffy,

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:56.393
but the teaching was fine.

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:01.620
And so I went to the
City College of New York

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:04.930
and got a Bachelor's
degree in Art Education

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:09.930
and then a Master of
Arts in Art Education.

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:14.240
And I am so glad that that happened

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:18.960
because I was able to teach children

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:21.470
and I hadn't planned to teach anything.

00:13:21.470 --> 00:13:23.073
I had planned to do art.

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:26.890
But this way I got a chance

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:29.510
to find out how wonderful
the children are.

00:13:29.510 --> 00:13:31.253
They are the best.

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:34.330
And for those of you who teach them,

00:13:34.330 --> 00:13:39.127
I'm sure you know what I
mean, the children, fabulous.

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:44.920
Now in 1948 I was 18 and I was a freshman

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:48.570
at the City College and we were learning

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:51.920
about all kinds of art done in Europe.

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:55.470
Like still life and so on.

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:57.180
And this was the first painting

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:02.020
that I really thought was good, you know,

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:04.370
because the teachers would
tear everything apart

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:06.880
but um yeah.
(audience laughing)

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:09.250
So this is my first oil on canvas

00:14:10.280 --> 00:14:11.113
and

00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:16.490
I went on

00:14:18.370 --> 00:14:20.170
what does that mean a different box?

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:24.320
Well it's not happening.

00:14:24.320 --> 00:14:27.630
What the heck?
(audience laughing)

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:29.800
Okay nothing is happening here.

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:34.449
I need to go on more
than 60 years making art.

00:14:34.449 --> 00:14:36.420
(audience laughing)

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:39.533
And all I'm getting is one piece.

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:45.240
I don't know why it's not moving.

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:46.343
I'm pushing buttons.

00:14:48.835 --> 00:14:51.210
(people chattering)

00:14:51.210 --> 00:14:52.305
- [Man] I can, help you do it.

00:14:52.305 --> 00:14:53.138
- [Faith] What is that, why is that?

00:14:53.138 --> 00:14:54.920
- [Man] Just let me know to
go to the next one, okay?

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:58.433
- [Faith] Yeah oh okay go okay here.

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:03.100
In one of my classes Design,

00:15:03.100 --> 00:15:05.020
oh we had a fabulous education

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:06.417
at the City College in art

00:15:06.417 --> 00:15:11.170
and I think they really did a
wonderful job on teaching us

00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:14.270
and one of the things that we learned

00:15:14.270 --> 00:15:17.590
to do very well was to create design.

00:15:17.590 --> 00:15:22.553
I mean all kinds from design
aspects from all over the world

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:28.530
and so okay here we got an assignment

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:30.213
to create a playing card.

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:35.580
Acrylic on paper playing card.

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:40.580
And most of the kids in the class

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:43.380
when we came back

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:46.440
and we went to show our
homework assignment,

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:50.520
they had created a whole deck of cards.

00:15:50.520 --> 00:15:52.330
I said wait what are you doing here?

00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:55.230
This is, it's not, you were not supposed

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:56.794
to make a deck of cards

00:15:56.794 --> 00:15:59.373
you're supposed to do just one.

00:16:00.940 --> 00:16:02.943
And they said we do what we want.

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:09.330
And that was their, it
was very competitive.

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:11.350
And of course the idea was

00:16:11.350 --> 00:16:13.220
they're gonna make a deck of cards

00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:15.370
and I'm gonna come in with my one

00:16:15.370 --> 00:16:17.120
and you know City College was free

00:16:18.290 --> 00:16:22.153
but not if you only gonna
do one and everybody else.

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:26.513
You're gonna be in trouble.

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:31.920
So I said okay that's the way
you all want to do it huh.

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:34.880
And I learned from that don't ever go in

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:36.657
with one of nothin'.

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:39.510
Make sure when you get in there,

00:16:39.510 --> 00:16:42.253
you can fill up the whole
board with your stuff.

00:16:44.182 --> 00:16:45.593
And I did.

00:16:47.630 --> 00:16:51.070
Yeah Playing Card 1948.

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:54.920
In the 1960s I became a mature artist

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:59.143
with the American People
Series and Black Light.

00:17:00.550 --> 00:17:04.870
The 1960s was a time when
things changed in America

00:17:04.870 --> 00:17:06.793
and will never be the same.

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:12.740
Much was for the better
however there seems

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:14.940
to be continuing longing

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:18.810
for the one step forward
two steps backward

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:23.810
that has been a time-worn
American legacy since the 1600s.

00:17:27.350 --> 00:17:30.710
I became a mature artist.

00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:31.823
Okay let's move on.

00:17:33.050 --> 00:17:36.417
Okay Self Portrait.

00:17:36.417 --> 00:17:40.480
I went into this whole series of painting

00:17:40.480 --> 00:17:44.600
with stop signs and circles

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:49.600
and that was very prevalent in those days.

00:17:50.830 --> 00:17:55.830
Pop Art was what they was saying it was

00:17:56.850 --> 00:18:01.850
and oil on stretched canvas
was my way of working

00:18:02.230 --> 00:18:07.230
and I am cradling myself for
the long haul ahead of me.

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:14.420
I was trying very hard to show my life

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:19.060
and to tell my story because to me

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:23.000
that is what an artist
is supposed to be doing,

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:25.084
telling their story.

00:18:25.084 --> 00:18:25.917
(cell phone ringing)

00:18:25.917 --> 00:18:27.334
What on earth?

00:18:27.334 --> 00:18:28.167
(audience laughing)

00:18:28.167 --> 00:18:29.000
What's going on.

00:18:32.382 --> 00:18:35.465
(audience laughing)

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:45.950
Art is important

00:18:46.060 --> 00:18:51.060
because you can tell
what happened to people

00:18:52.280 --> 00:18:57.280
all over the world at
any time in their history

00:18:57.410 --> 00:18:59.273
from looking at their art.

00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:02.080
And I wanted to be a part of that.

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:06.760
I wanted to help to tell the
story of African-Americans

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:11.760
in America at the time when I
was there, when I was alive.

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:17.790
And I got that opportunity to do that

00:19:17.790 --> 00:19:19.360
by becoming an artist.

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:21.810
This is the American People Series

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:26.810
and which was started in
1963 and went on to 1967.

00:19:29.220 --> 00:19:32.760
This is actually an image of my brother

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:35.840
who died very early in his life

00:19:36.749 --> 00:19:39.060
and I wanted to always remember him.

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:43.440
He was such a sweetheart but
things were rough in those days

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:47.453
and he couldn't make
it, he didn't make it.

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:52.860
My poor brother gone before his time.

00:19:52.860 --> 00:19:55.690
I think I need to go faster

00:19:55.690 --> 00:19:58.633
but I don't know how
to get that happening.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.000
I became an artist because
I wanted to tell my story

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:05.870
as a Black Woman in America.

00:20:05.870 --> 00:20:08.620
We were in the height of
the civil rights movement

00:20:08.620 --> 00:20:12.110
and I wanted my art to be
a witness to the changes

00:20:12.110 --> 00:20:13.700
that were taking place.

00:20:13.700 --> 00:20:17.610
It was then that I announced
my arrival on the art scene

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:21.853
as no longer emerging, I never
liked that word emerging,

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:28.180
I was now a mature artist if not me who,

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:32.020
if not now when?

00:20:32.020 --> 00:20:37.020
In 1967 I had my first solo
show at the Spectrum Gallery

00:20:37.450 --> 00:20:40.653
on 57th Street in New York City.

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:47.920
And here I am standing
between friends under the,

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:54.430
actually this is across

00:20:56.740 --> 00:20:59.620
something linking women together

00:20:59.620 --> 00:21:04.370
so that there would be

00:21:04.370 --> 00:21:09.370
some kind of mutuality between us

00:21:09.470 --> 00:21:12.403
and not so much hate and destruction.

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:17.210
This is the first painting in
the American People series.

00:21:17.210 --> 00:21:22.210
I did in the summer a
lot of parties to raise,

00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:25.810
doing a lot of parties to raise money

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:29.650
for the civil rights
causes like the NAACP.

00:21:29.650 --> 00:21:34.430
I was staying with my
daughters and my husband

00:21:34.430 --> 00:21:39.430
in what Provincetown and
it was very interesting

00:21:39.650 --> 00:21:41.470
what was going on there.

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:44.480
The people there were
colorful and interesting,

00:21:44.480 --> 00:21:46.780
their images were unforgettable.

00:21:46.780 --> 00:21:49.940
I almost never forget a face.

00:21:49.940 --> 00:21:52.570
They often show up in my paintings.

00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:53.853
Yes I'm still looking.

00:21:55.100 --> 00:21:57.913
And things are getting
more and more interesting.

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:01.490
For Members Only.

00:22:01.490 --> 00:22:03.670
We were trying to go to a,

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:06.363
what are we trying to get to, a park.

00:22:07.760 --> 00:22:12.760
This is a what a church school picnic

00:22:13.130 --> 00:22:15.210
and these guys showed up to tell us,

00:22:15.210 --> 00:22:17.510
you know you're not supposed to be here.

00:22:17.510 --> 00:22:18.590
A lot of racism,

00:22:18.590 --> 00:22:23.590
a lot of prejudice in
different neighborhoods

00:22:24.110 --> 00:22:27.340
and so on and so I got
a chance to experience

00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:32.340
all these things and to feel
the power of being an artist

00:22:32.760 --> 00:22:37.640
to record what I saw, For Members Only.

00:22:37.640 --> 00:22:39.960
Well you had to be a member.

00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:44.290
Well I couldn't be a member to
all these things, absolutely.

00:22:44.290 --> 00:22:45.750
They wanted us to leave.

00:22:45.750 --> 00:22:47.020
We left.

00:22:47.020 --> 00:22:49.703
Have you ever had an experience like that?

00:22:50.670 --> 00:22:54.710
Well actually it never
happened to me again

00:22:54.710 --> 00:22:57.293
but I did have that first one.

00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:01.396
They told us to get the hell out of there.

00:23:01.396 --> 00:23:02.450
(audience laughing)

00:23:02.450 --> 00:23:07.093
Neighbors, a lot of racism in the schools

00:23:09.140 --> 00:23:11.120
and in the neighborhoods

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:14.310
and all with people not wanting you

00:23:14.310 --> 00:23:17.093
to live next door or whatever.

00:23:17.930 --> 00:23:21.830
Could you make a painting of people

00:23:21.830 --> 00:23:24.373
who wouldn't want you to live with them?

00:23:28.090 --> 00:23:29.073
Would you want to?

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:36.203
A family of people who
want to keep it their way.

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:40.820
These things have been
difficult through the years

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:45.640
and I have tried to do what I can.

00:23:45.640 --> 00:23:49.193
Could have that click again?

00:23:50.740 --> 00:23:53.053
The Civil Rights Triangle.

00:23:54.550 --> 00:23:57.650
You know the president of the NAACP

00:23:57.650 --> 00:23:59.943
back in those days was not black.

00:24:01.384 --> 00:24:03.230
I don't know whether you know that or not

00:24:03.230 --> 00:24:08.050
but that's what these
the triangle is about

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:12.303
and I was right in the middle of it.

00:24:16.401 --> 00:24:19.000
And in that place where
we went Martha's Vineyard

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:20.920
where my mother had a friend

00:24:20.920 --> 00:24:23.480
and I started these paintings

00:24:25.080 --> 00:24:29.500
because they were very
important members of NAACP

00:24:30.460 --> 00:24:32.950
but there's a lot going on

00:24:32.950 --> 00:24:36.160
and I wanted to record
my feelings about it.

00:24:36.160 --> 00:24:37.483
Watching and Waiting.

00:24:38.780 --> 00:24:41.650
People had to you know wait their turns

00:24:41.650 --> 00:24:45.760
to see if they could
participate in what is going.

00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:49.810
Who is the man at the door?

00:24:49.810 --> 00:24:50.920
He's standing there waiting

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:53.060
for his chance to to be heard,

00:24:53.060 --> 00:24:57.093
to sit at the table, to participate.

00:24:57.950 --> 00:24:59.670
Who was watching and waiting?

00:24:59.670 --> 00:25:00.693
What for?

00:25:02.070 --> 00:25:06.233
Okay this was Mr. Charlie.

00:25:09.690 --> 00:25:14.500
I think the idea was is that
he was a nice guy so come on.

00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:18.963
Red, blue and white arrows
pointing downward suggested

00:25:19.920 --> 00:25:22.860
that things were not
gonna be all that great.

00:25:22.860 --> 00:25:27.860
Stop signs and all and
Mr. Charlie, Mr. Charlie,

00:25:28.170 --> 00:25:33.170
see this man's hand on
his heart and his smile,

00:25:34.300 --> 00:25:35.923
red stop sign,

00:25:36.960 --> 00:25:41.413
red, blue and white
arrows pointing downwards.

00:25:42.490 --> 00:25:43.763
Is he sincere?

00:25:44.670 --> 00:25:48.680
I think generally he might have been

00:25:50.180 --> 00:25:53.223
but Mr. Charlie was big in 1964.

00:25:54.070 --> 00:25:57.690
Okay 63.

00:25:57.690 --> 00:26:00.590
And here is a whole pile up of guys

00:26:00.590 --> 00:26:03.173
with their hands over
one of them sad face,

00:26:04.873 --> 00:26:08.013
why are these men piled
up on top of each other?

00:26:13.690 --> 00:26:17.943
What does the position
of their hands tell us?

00:26:18.790 --> 00:26:21.523
It's a way of keeping you down.

00:26:22.590 --> 00:26:24.373
Who's the man on top?

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:27.420
How will you stay there?

00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:29.400
That is an interesting question

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:33.470
that America continues to battle with.

00:26:33.470 --> 00:26:35.600
Some people want to be on top

00:26:36.610 --> 00:26:38.830
and they will do anything to stay there

00:26:40.450 --> 00:26:41.283
and

00:26:44.520 --> 00:26:48.530
it is unfortunate that we have still

00:26:50.560 --> 00:26:52.670
so much going on

00:26:52.670 --> 00:26:56.470
that prevents people from prospering,

00:27:03.070 --> 00:27:06.360
getting together and prospering.

00:27:06.360 --> 00:27:08.023
The American dream.

00:27:09.030 --> 00:27:11.990
All women want a ring like that

00:27:11.990 --> 00:27:14.800
and you know a guy to go with it.

00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:19.660
Yeah that's it, that's
tough sorry. (laughing)

00:27:19.660 --> 00:27:21.890
How can you achieve it?

00:27:21.890 --> 00:27:24.033
Well we find a way don't we?

00:27:24.880 --> 00:27:27.433
Yeah yes I think so.

00:27:29.290 --> 00:27:30.860
The American Dream, all right.

00:27:31.790 --> 00:27:35.957
I just made a you know black
white whatever Study Now.

00:27:38.701 --> 00:27:40.470
Ah that was important.

00:27:40.470 --> 00:27:44.880
A lot of young kids got
into various schools

00:27:44.880 --> 00:27:49.880
that had previously prohibited
black children going there.

00:27:50.430 --> 00:27:51.360
They would get in there

00:27:51.360 --> 00:27:56.100
and then they would forget
about doing the work

00:27:56.100 --> 00:27:58.793
and they would flunk out.

00:28:00.380 --> 00:28:02.480
Study Now.

00:28:02.480 --> 00:28:03.853
Don't forget.

00:28:05.110 --> 00:28:08.270
This painting is a message to students.

00:28:08.270 --> 00:28:10.403
See the four caution signs?

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:15.943
See the red arrow and yellow
red and black stop sign.

00:28:16.900 --> 00:28:20.047
Why should you Study Now?

00:28:20.047 --> 00:28:23.130
'Cause if you don't you're
gonna get thrown out

00:28:23.130 --> 00:28:26.203
and that's not gonna be
pleasant for anybody.

00:28:28.940 --> 00:28:32.340
Three Men on a Fence trying to decide

00:28:32.340 --> 00:28:33.540
which way should they go

00:28:33.540 --> 00:28:34.490
what should they do

00:28:35.410 --> 00:28:37.570
should they do this?

00:28:37.570 --> 00:28:40.793
Could you make a picture
about what you did?

00:28:42.020 --> 00:28:45.533
Have you ever sat on the fence?

00:28:46.920 --> 00:28:49.853
Could you make a picture
about what you did?

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:52.963
Look back on it.

00:28:54.100 --> 00:28:55.713
The Family Plan.

00:28:57.630 --> 00:29:02.223
Some parents wanted their
schools kept segregated.

00:29:03.220 --> 00:29:06.640
They had a plan to keep them segregated.

00:29:06.640 --> 00:29:10.120
For the most part their plan worked.

00:29:10.120 --> 00:29:13.793
Could you make a picture
of the kids at your school?

00:29:15.810 --> 00:29:17.700
Schools are still quite segregated

00:29:17.700 --> 00:29:21.600
and they do it through the
neighborhoods being segregated.

00:29:21.600 --> 00:29:24.853
Keep this neighborhood
segregated the schools can do it.

00:29:28.250 --> 00:29:29.643
God Bless America.

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:35.180
This was my very first flag painting.

00:29:35.180 --> 00:29:39.863
I was trying to make a
picture that looked patriotic.

00:29:41.870 --> 00:29:43.253
Does she look patriotic?

00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:46.143
What is patriotism?

00:29:47.470 --> 00:29:51.740
All right yeah I wanted to do that.

00:29:51.740 --> 00:29:54.560
Portrait of an American Youth.

00:29:54.560 --> 00:29:57.863
I think I already showed this one.

00:29:59.980 --> 00:30:01.740
Stop the red stop sign.

00:30:01.740 --> 00:30:03.160
What does this mean?

00:30:03.160 --> 00:30:05.000
Who is this young man?

00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:06.620
He reminds me of my brother

00:30:08.320 --> 00:30:12.080
where his life was sort of set up for him.

00:30:12.080 --> 00:30:13.950
Two of these girls are my daughters,

00:30:13.950 --> 00:30:17.630
Barbara and Michelle, who
are the other children?

00:30:17.630 --> 00:30:19.780
Well they're kids they
went to school with.

00:30:21.010 --> 00:30:24.710
Why are they hiding and
from whom are they hiding?

00:30:24.710 --> 00:30:27.253
Or are they just simply playing?

00:30:28.230 --> 00:30:30.110
I wanted to show them playing,

00:30:30.110 --> 00:30:34.990
trying to be friends
trying to avoid the racism

00:30:34.990 --> 00:30:37.880
that followed children, that follows,

00:30:37.880 --> 00:30:40.583
continues to follow children everywhere.

00:30:43.780 --> 00:30:46.030
Woman Looking in a Mirror.

00:30:46.030 --> 00:30:47.323
Okay yeah.

00:30:48.700 --> 00:30:51.840
See the watercolor

00:30:53.330 --> 00:30:56.770
which the plants

00:30:56.770 --> 00:30:58.660
with the plants outside

00:30:58.660 --> 00:31:03.650
and the moon what feeling through,

00:31:05.590 --> 00:31:07.870
peeking through the leaves.

00:31:07.870 --> 00:31:11.470
This is a beautiful night
and she is a beautiful woman.

00:31:11.470 --> 00:31:15.410
Can you paint a woman
looking in the mirror

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:19.060
and who is she by the way?

00:31:19.060 --> 00:31:20.500
Is she you?

00:31:20.500 --> 00:31:22.530
Yeah, this was fun to do.

00:31:22.530 --> 00:31:24.160
The Artist and His Model.

00:31:24.160 --> 00:31:25.830
There were a lot of artists

00:31:26.980 --> 00:31:29.960
at that time who thought they had an idea

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:34.813
about what kind of people I should paint.

00:31:34.813 --> 00:31:38.000
Don't paint this, don't paint that.

00:31:38.000 --> 00:31:38.873
I do what I want.

00:31:40.680 --> 00:31:43.750
Oh yeah and there was imagery

00:31:44.910 --> 00:31:47.980
that they thought was perfect and correct

00:31:47.980 --> 00:31:50.230
and imagery that they thought was not

00:31:50.230 --> 00:31:52.080
and I thought I could do what I want.

00:31:53.020 --> 00:31:57.100
What do you think of
this ongoing debate now.

00:31:57.100 --> 00:32:01.092
Black or just American?

00:32:01.092 --> 00:32:02.700
It was a lot of discussion

00:32:02.700 --> 00:32:04.810
about whether you should pay black people

00:32:06.580 --> 00:32:08.800
or who are the black people?

00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:13.700
Well I can decide and
I can make what I want

00:32:14.880 --> 00:32:16.913
and I did.

00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:20.340
Why is our flag bleeding?

00:32:20.340 --> 00:32:23.030
Yeah the flag is bleeding.

00:32:23.030 --> 00:32:24.253
Why is it bleeding?

00:32:25.825 --> 00:32:30.317
(sighs) It's bleeding
because our liberties,

00:32:32.130 --> 00:32:36.360
are damaged to some extents,

00:32:36.360 --> 00:32:40.913
our freedom is in trouble.

00:32:43.890 --> 00:32:48.110
It was so difficult painting that blood

00:32:48.110 --> 00:32:51.440
and if you've never tried
painting blood try it.

00:32:51.440 --> 00:32:54.513
It really feels dangerous.

00:32:56.650 --> 00:33:01.650
Then it all of a sudden in 1967
somebody yelled Black Power.

00:33:04.240 --> 00:33:07.453
Oh my God everybody got very upset.

00:33:08.894 --> 00:33:09.727
Black Power?

00:33:09.727 --> 00:33:13.060
I mean Black and power,
these two don't go together.

00:33:13.060 --> 00:33:15.490
Yeah something's wrong.

00:33:15.490 --> 00:33:20.340
And you see here, what do you see written

00:33:20.340 --> 00:33:22.460
in the horizontal?

00:33:22.460 --> 00:33:25.090
Put your head on the right.

00:33:25.090 --> 00:33:25.923
What do you see?

00:33:27.230 --> 00:33:29.483
All right that's the power.

00:33:30.420 --> 00:33:32.210
And the Black Power was something

00:33:32.210 --> 00:33:36.150
that somebody yelled out.

00:33:36.150 --> 00:33:36.983
Who was it?

00:33:39.035 --> 00:33:39.868
Pardon me?

00:33:41.670 --> 00:33:43.490
Stokely Carmichael.

00:33:43.490 --> 00:33:47.100
Yeah that was a very big
day when that happened.

00:33:47.100 --> 00:33:48.050
What happened here?

00:33:48.976 --> 00:33:51.485
(audience laughing)

00:33:51.485 --> 00:33:53.873
Oh I'm doin' it. (laughing)

00:33:55.160 --> 00:33:56.883
Okay oh my God that's funny.

00:33:57.900 --> 00:34:02.900
Oh yeah okay the Black Light Series.

00:34:05.640 --> 00:34:09.790
Okay now this is I've done, what?

00:34:09.790 --> 00:34:10.653
What's going on?

00:34:11.504 --> 00:34:12.337
(people chattering)

00:34:12.337 --> 00:34:13.170
You got me.

00:34:14.030 --> 00:34:18.580
Well this is my computer so now

00:34:18.580 --> 00:34:23.430
and this is the subway on 125th Street

00:34:24.623 --> 00:34:29.623
and this is this whole
series the Black Light Series

00:34:30.380 --> 00:34:34.960
in which I wanted to
change my use of colors

00:34:34.960 --> 00:34:39.110
and then Die which was the last

00:34:39.110 --> 00:34:43.130
of the American People Series was Die.

00:34:43.130 --> 00:34:43.963
Huh?

00:34:44.990 --> 00:34:49.290
Which is now in the collection
of the Museum of Modern Art

00:34:50.840 --> 00:34:55.840
after 50 years, I did it 50 years ago,

00:34:57.270 --> 00:35:01.610
and they now have it
because I loved Picasso

00:35:01.610 --> 00:35:04.220
and his Guernica.

00:35:04.220 --> 00:35:07.350
And we used to go there, used
to take my daughter's there

00:35:07.350 --> 00:35:10.000
and we would look at Guernica and so on

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:15.000
and in my series of American
People I thought it would I,

00:35:15.090 --> 00:35:18.950
I wanted to do a spontaneous street riot.

00:35:18.950 --> 00:35:20.700
Because there was so
many, they would happen.

00:35:20.700 --> 00:35:24.940
Just I mean it spontaneously
a riot would break out.

00:35:24.940 --> 00:35:26.700
And you would be in the middle of it.

00:35:26.700 --> 00:35:29.420
You had to get the hell out of the way

00:35:29.420 --> 00:35:31.750
and blood would be in the street

00:35:31.750 --> 00:35:36.750
and when you got home
nobody would have seen it

00:35:37.020 --> 00:35:38.670
on television and it wouldn't be

00:35:38.670 --> 00:35:40.170
in the newspaper the next day.

00:35:40.170 --> 00:35:42.110
They just act like it didn't happen.

00:35:42.110 --> 00:35:43.003
Amazing.

00:35:44.650 --> 00:35:49.050
And so I thought I'm gonna do a painting

00:35:49.050 --> 00:35:51.820
showing a spontaneous street riot

00:35:52.690 --> 00:35:55.790
and it was inspired by Guernica
which I used to look at

00:35:55.790 --> 00:35:59.143
all the time because it
was a riotous painting.

00:36:04.300 --> 00:36:08.507
And I tried to, what was it,

00:36:10.980 --> 00:36:15.310
Chase Manhattan Bank David
Rockefeller Center Group,

00:36:15.310 --> 00:36:20.310
to my studio, to buy my first what?

00:36:20.860 --> 00:36:25.860
My first painting from a bank from Chase.

00:36:27.500 --> 00:36:30.460
And I showed them those
paintings that you just saw

00:36:31.400 --> 00:36:34.183
and they just said oh my God,

00:36:34.183 --> 00:36:37.120
(audience laughing)

00:36:37.120 --> 00:36:39.293
we can't take any more we have to go.

00:36:41.380 --> 00:36:44.390
And okay so.

00:36:44.390 --> 00:36:48.370
But I knew that David Rockefeller
was gonna send him back

00:36:48.370 --> 00:36:49.860
so go ahead.

00:36:49.860 --> 00:36:51.790
So they went and when they came back,

00:36:51.790 --> 00:36:54.607
I showed them the Blacklight Series.

00:36:59.170 --> 00:37:00.405
What was that?

00:37:00.405 --> 00:37:01.505
Could you go back one?

00:37:02.990 --> 00:37:05.633
Wait, yeah, there we go.

00:37:09.220 --> 00:37:10.990
And they wanted to buy this.

00:37:10.990 --> 00:37:13.687
And my mother said, "Okay listen now look.

00:37:13.687 --> 00:37:16.777
"They're coming back,
they're gonna buy something.

00:37:16.777 --> 00:37:18.127
"Show him something they can buy.

00:37:18.127 --> 00:37:21.890
"It's a think about it it's a bank okay?

00:37:21.890 --> 00:37:25.247
"It's a bank you gotta you
know think about that."

00:37:26.370 --> 00:37:27.763
And so I did.

00:37:29.410 --> 00:37:31.107
And they liked it

00:37:33.360 --> 00:37:34.830
and they said

00:37:36.440 --> 00:37:38.930
they call it the American Spectrum.

00:37:38.930 --> 00:37:41.413
No I called it the American Spectrum.

00:37:42.470 --> 00:37:44.403
They wanted to call it what?

00:37:46.100 --> 00:37:47.920
They had an idea

00:37:49.840 --> 00:37:50.673
to call it,

00:37:50.673 --> 00:37:52.200
I was trying to show America

00:37:52.200 --> 00:37:54.203
with all its different shades of people.

00:37:55.210 --> 00:37:59.317
And anyway they bought it.

00:37:59.317 --> 00:38:00.601
(audience laughing)

00:38:00.601 --> 00:38:01.530
That's the key.

00:38:01.530 --> 00:38:04.120
Now titles mean a lot in art.

00:38:04.120 --> 00:38:08.820
You know you gotta be
very aware of the fact

00:38:08.820 --> 00:38:12.823
that many people buy a
painting based on the title.

00:38:13.750 --> 00:38:16.690
Very interesting I thought.

00:38:16.690 --> 00:38:20.010
And for years I used to go to the Chase

00:38:20.010 --> 00:38:24.130
and right outside the
president's office would be

00:38:24.130 --> 00:38:25.990
that painting hanging there.

00:38:25.990 --> 00:38:27.923
And that was nice, I liked it.

00:38:28.870 --> 00:38:33.870
By 1967 we had reached
the Black is Beautiful,

00:38:33.930 --> 00:38:35.700
Black Pride,

00:38:35.700 --> 00:38:39.240
Black Power segments

00:38:39.240 --> 00:38:42.223
of the civil rights movement of the 1960s.

00:38:43.240 --> 00:38:46.110
I wanted to create art

00:38:46.110 --> 00:38:51.110
to show the Black is Beautiful
skin tones of black people.

00:38:51.230 --> 00:38:55.090
We black people had never
been called beautiful before

00:38:55.090 --> 00:38:57.830
but it was a raging Black is Beautiful.

00:38:57.830 --> 00:38:59.580
Okay I'll go for it.

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:01.820
A black image in a black background

00:39:01.820 --> 00:39:06.820
like a white image in a
white background is invisible

00:39:06.940 --> 00:39:09.590
without tonal variations.

00:39:09.590 --> 00:39:13.280
So my paintings would
be dark but not black.

00:39:13.280 --> 00:39:17.840
I had to add some small amount
of white pigment to my colors

00:39:17.840 --> 00:39:19.990
to give them opacity.

00:39:19.990 --> 00:39:23.530
Then I could add the black
pigment to darken my colors.

00:39:23.530 --> 00:39:24.363
This was wonderful.

00:39:24.363 --> 00:39:26.120
I love working this way.

00:39:26.120 --> 00:39:29.780
It was magic how dark colors looked black

00:39:29.780 --> 00:39:31.470
against the white canvas.

00:39:31.470 --> 00:39:35.020
But when placed next to each other,

00:39:35.020 --> 00:39:38.420
dark dull colors were clearly visible.

00:39:38.420 --> 00:39:40.820
You could see them much better.

00:39:40.820 --> 00:39:42.880
I was entranced with the results.

00:39:42.880 --> 00:39:45.150
I created the following twelve canvases

00:39:45.150 --> 00:39:49.770
which I called Black Light:
Black Paintings of Black People

00:39:51.500 --> 00:39:56.200
And this one Big Black was the first one.

00:39:56.200 --> 00:40:01.200
Yeah that is the presence of all color.

00:40:03.510 --> 00:40:07.210
White is the absence of color.

00:40:07.210 --> 00:40:12.080
And I worked with those ideas in my color

00:40:13.100 --> 00:40:16.030
for many years and I still do.

00:40:16.030 --> 00:40:19.900
Except I stopped working in oil paint

00:40:19.900 --> 00:40:22.760
because I was allergic, you know.

00:40:22.760 --> 00:40:27.760
Yeah it's a lot of artists
got sick from using oil paint

00:40:27.980 --> 00:40:32.980
and an acrylic paint
became very important.

00:40:35.590 --> 00:40:38.150
And so I went on and on in order

00:40:38.150 --> 00:40:43.150
to make and create clearly
defined abstract shapes.

00:40:43.740 --> 00:40:44.980
Can you make a face

00:40:44.980 --> 00:40:48.640
by using abstract shapes and flat colors?

00:40:48.640 --> 00:40:49.473
Try it.

00:40:51.270 --> 00:40:53.650
Black Light I called it.

00:40:53.650 --> 00:40:54.743
Soul Sister.

00:40:56.890 --> 00:40:59.260
I can't think of a more liberating time

00:40:59.260 --> 00:41:01.710
in my life than the 1960s.

00:41:01.710 --> 00:41:05.140
It was then that I learned
to wear my hair natural.

00:41:05.140 --> 00:41:06.970
Oh that's when the natural hair,

00:41:06.970 --> 00:41:10.660
no more hot combs in my hair.

00:41:10.660 --> 00:41:12.420
Black is Beautiful, Black Pride

00:41:12.420 --> 00:41:16.230
and Black Power all in one gesture.

00:41:16.230 --> 00:41:21.230
Now everybody's got their
hair all wow. (laughing)

00:41:23.400 --> 00:41:24.900
Don't have to worry about that anymore.

00:41:24.900 --> 00:41:26.170
What is it?

00:41:26.170 --> 00:41:27.003
- [Woman] You got five minutes.

00:41:27.003 --> 00:41:27.971
- [Faith] You got five what?

00:41:27.971 --> 00:41:30.040
Excuse me?
- Five minutes.

00:41:30.040 --> 00:41:33.143
- [Faith] Five minutes,
well let's see what you got.

00:41:34.141 --> 00:41:35.650
(audience laughing)

00:41:35.650 --> 00:41:37.833
Mommy and Daddy let's move on.

00:41:39.640 --> 00:41:43.510
More Black Light Mommy and Daddy 1969.

00:41:43.510 --> 00:41:45.483
keep go, who's who's got the thing?

00:41:47.270 --> 00:41:48.830
- [Man] Just tell me
next and I'll move it.

00:41:48.830 --> 00:41:49.880
- [Faith] Okay, next.

00:41:51.123 --> 00:41:54.064
(people chattering)

00:41:54.064 --> 00:41:55.688
And there's Die.

00:41:55.688 --> 00:41:56.950
- [Man] I got it here.

00:41:56.950 --> 00:41:59.201
- [Faith] Oh here can I push that?

00:41:59.201 --> 00:42:00.178
Where can I push.

00:42:00.178 --> 00:42:03.560
(man chattering)

00:42:03.560 --> 00:42:05.466
- [Man] You can push right
here it's this button.

00:42:05.466 --> 00:42:06.299
- [Faith] Okay good.

00:42:06.299 --> 00:42:08.750
- And that will go.
- Okay, great.

00:42:08.750 --> 00:42:10.130
Black Art Poster.

00:42:10.130 --> 00:42:12.010
Oh the Black Art Poster.

00:42:12.010 --> 00:42:15.630
The Schomburg library got
that because it's a library

00:42:15.630 --> 00:42:18.600
at home and they didn't
own anything of mine.

00:42:18.600 --> 00:42:19.743
So I gave 'em that.

00:42:21.100 --> 00:42:25.720
And I was in there just
recently looking for it.

00:42:25.720 --> 00:42:30.720
You know it's very difficult
to find that I gotta go back

00:42:33.713 --> 00:42:34.920
and find it.

00:42:34.920 --> 00:42:37.330
Can you make a picture to express yourself

00:42:37.330 --> 00:42:39.083
without name-calling.

00:42:40.190 --> 00:42:41.640
Okay.

00:42:41.640 --> 00:42:45.510
I don't know red nigger, white nigger, oh,

00:42:45.510 --> 00:42:49.750
there was a lot of niggers
yelling in those days

00:42:49.750 --> 00:42:53.180
and I wanted

00:42:53.180 --> 00:42:55.960
to say it's not appropriate.

00:42:55.960 --> 00:43:00.200
Unless you are a nigger don't say nigger.

00:43:00.200 --> 00:43:04.283
Okay you're one, okay,
not one don't do it.

00:43:06.090 --> 00:43:07.950
The American Spectrum.

00:43:07.950 --> 00:43:10.770
Okay this is the one
that the Chase bought.

00:43:10.770 --> 00:43:12.513
Flag for the Moon: Die Nigger.

00:43:13.680 --> 00:43:15.640
I'm not sure who owns that now.

00:43:15.640 --> 00:43:16.663
Somebody does.

00:43:17.550 --> 00:43:18.631
You remember?

00:43:18.631 --> 00:43:19.910
(woman chattering faintly)

00:43:19.910 --> 00:43:20.743
what?

00:43:21.600 --> 00:43:22.590
Oh it is okay.

00:43:22.590 --> 00:43:23.423
(people chattering)

00:43:23.423 --> 00:43:26.480
Uh, oh. (laughing)

00:43:26.480 --> 00:43:29.193
Silver Stan yeah.

00:43:31.070 --> 00:43:32.233
All right here we are.

00:43:33.320 --> 00:43:35.440
US America Black.

00:43:35.440 --> 00:43:37.980
I just wanted to do party time,

00:43:37.980 --> 00:43:39.440
I wanted people dancing.

00:43:39.440 --> 00:43:40.846
The 1970s.

00:43:40.846 --> 00:43:45.380
In the 1970s I discovered
my roots in African art

00:43:45.380 --> 00:43:49.850
and began to paint and create art specific

00:43:49.850 --> 00:43:52.250
to my identity as a black woman.

00:43:52.250 --> 00:43:56.870
I made dolls and and masks inspired

00:43:56.870 --> 00:43:57.703
by my painting.

00:43:59.030 --> 00:44:00.310
I began to write

00:44:01.210 --> 00:44:03.260
Oh no, I did this for the Woman's House.

00:44:03.260 --> 00:44:08.260
I got Singer Center for the Woman's House.

00:44:08.930 --> 00:44:10.480
Yeah oil on canvas.

00:44:10.480 --> 00:44:13.500
Well the men thought, they
didn't like it very much

00:44:13.500 --> 00:44:14.770
when they moved in

00:44:14.770 --> 00:44:19.200
and the women moved to
the the Woman's House

00:44:20.850 --> 00:44:22.960
and they painted over
it in White House paint.

00:44:22.960 --> 00:44:24.529
That was a problem.

00:44:24.529 --> 00:44:26.670
Yeah that wasn't nice.

00:44:26.670 --> 00:44:28.150
They said they got tired of listening,

00:44:28.150 --> 00:44:29.600
looking at those bitches.

00:44:29.600 --> 00:44:30.753
Okay.

00:44:30.753 --> 00:44:31.586
(crowd muttering)

00:44:31.586 --> 00:44:34.640
Yeah so but, I called

00:44:42.160 --> 00:44:45.290
the head of the prison center

00:44:45.290 --> 00:44:49.330
and he had raised $25,000

00:44:49.330 --> 00:44:52.260
and had that painting restored.

00:44:52.260 --> 00:44:54.243
And now you would never know

00:44:54.243 --> 00:44:57.180
that it had been painted
over with White House paint

00:44:57.180 --> 00:45:00.520
and it is now going to
be in the collection

00:45:00.520 --> 00:45:02.090
of the Brooklyn Museum

00:45:02.090 --> 00:45:04.217
not going back to the prison.

00:45:04.217 --> 00:45:05.424
- No.
- No.

00:45:05.424 --> 00:45:07.633
- [Faith] No, yeah that was hard.

00:45:09.180 --> 00:45:12.230
Okay let's see what's going on.

00:45:12.230 --> 00:45:15.920
Oh my God I have done
so many different kinds

00:45:15.920 --> 00:45:20.920
of all kinds of painting
and these are the Tankas

00:45:23.510 --> 00:45:24.560
that I discovered.

00:45:24.560 --> 00:45:27.070
And these are my dolls

00:45:27.070 --> 00:45:30.193
which I do actually discovered
making dolls in Africa.

00:45:31.430 --> 00:45:34.318
And here is the mosaics.

00:45:34.318 --> 00:45:37.140
(audience laughing)

00:45:37.140 --> 00:45:39.150
Harlem Heroes then hell what?

00:45:39.150 --> 00:45:42.070
- You should go back.
- Go back where?

00:45:42.070 --> 00:45:43.487
Oh go back where.

00:45:44.510 --> 00:45:46.313
Okay.
- Let's go back to.

00:45:48.640 --> 00:45:51.123
- [Faith] Okay here?

00:45:52.550 --> 00:45:54.370
- Next.
- Yes.

00:45:54.370 --> 00:45:59.370
Masks and dolls are really
fun and I loved making them.

00:46:00.360 --> 00:46:03.303
I like to work in different media.

00:46:04.400 --> 00:46:06.260
We Flew Over the Bridge Memoir.

00:46:06.260 --> 00:46:11.040
Oh I wrote my memoir in
1980 had it published

00:46:11.040 --> 00:46:14.820
and it was very difficult
getting that to happen.

00:46:14.820 --> 00:46:18.830
And then the masks, Freedom
of Speech commissioned

00:46:18.830 --> 00:46:20.800
by the Constitution Center

00:46:20.800 --> 00:46:22.870
to celebrate the 200th anniversary

00:46:22.870 --> 00:46:24.470
of the Bill of Rights

00:46:24.470 --> 00:46:27.793
and it is in the collection of the museum.

00:46:29.210 --> 00:46:31.030
Oh the Metropolitan Museum of Art.

00:46:31.030 --> 00:46:35.163
Here's the subway in Harlem.

00:46:36.380 --> 00:46:41.380
Yeah so I've had a lot of
opportunities to do many things

00:46:41.940 --> 00:46:46.710
and the idea is when you
get an opportunity do it

00:46:46.710 --> 00:46:49.260
and don't turn down any opportunities.

00:46:49.260 --> 00:46:52.500
That's one thing that's
very important to learn

00:46:52.500 --> 00:46:53.943
as you're in college.

00:46:55.330 --> 00:46:58.733
Don't decide that you shouldn't do it.

00:47:00.150 --> 00:47:04.870
Yeah this is 125th Street
and what Lenox Avenue.

00:47:04.870 --> 00:47:05.720
Yeah.

00:47:05.720 --> 00:47:09.263
These series of People Portraits.

00:47:11.469 --> 00:47:16.469
The 52 mosaics was done for
the Civic Center in California.

00:47:19.430 --> 00:47:20.950
That was fun.

00:47:20.950 --> 00:47:23.420
52 images.

00:47:23.420 --> 00:47:24.830
Huh?

00:47:24.830 --> 00:47:29.830
Yeah I wanted to create
imagery of people walking

00:47:30.470 --> 00:47:34.460
in the subway all
different kinds of people,

00:47:34.460 --> 00:47:39.460
dancing, performing,
playing music, whatever,

00:47:42.700 --> 00:47:45.583
playing different sports.

00:47:47.770 --> 00:47:48.603
Yeah.

00:47:49.870 --> 00:47:54.140
And I won that Commission,
Mosaics Commission in California.

00:47:55.620 --> 00:47:59.360
So if you're ever in L.A. go see it.

00:47:59.360 --> 00:48:00.757
52 images.

00:48:01.970 --> 00:48:05.080
Yeah making mosaics is really fun.

00:48:05.080 --> 00:48:08.424
I painted images and mother did the,

00:48:08.424 --> 00:48:11.463
oh this is Echoes of
Harlem, my first quilt.

00:48:12.762 --> 00:48:16.490
I started writing on my second one,

00:48:16.490 --> 00:48:18.160
Who's Afraid of Aunt Jemima?

00:48:18.160 --> 00:48:20.000
See the story I wrote.

00:48:20.000 --> 00:48:21.410
And I wrote that story

00:48:21.410 --> 00:48:23.070
because I had written my autobiography

00:48:23.070 --> 00:48:24.580
and couldn't get it published.

00:48:24.580 --> 00:48:26.143
And I said I know what I'll do.

00:48:27.750 --> 00:48:31.630
I'll write on my art and
when they see the art

00:48:31.630 --> 00:48:33.853
they'll read the story, that I can do.

00:48:35.131 --> 00:48:38.260
I mean I will not allow somebody
to decide what my story is.

00:48:38.260 --> 00:48:39.263
I will write it.

00:48:40.150 --> 00:48:43.640
Sony's Quilt and on and on.

00:48:43.640 --> 00:48:44.993
Woman on the Bridge.

00:48:49.330 --> 00:48:51.280
This is at Crystal Bridges right now,

00:48:51.280 --> 00:48:55.263
Museum of American Art,
Bentonville, Arkansas.

00:48:56.452 --> 00:48:57.440
Here I am with

00:49:00.140 --> 00:49:01.760
- Oprah.
- Oprah.

00:49:01.760 --> 00:49:04.390
- and Maya.
- And Maya.

00:49:04.390 --> 00:49:09.390
Oprah commissioned this
painting for me to do.

00:49:11.260 --> 00:49:14.610
Maya that was really nice of her

00:49:14.610 --> 00:49:18.720
but she doesn't believe

00:49:18.720 --> 00:49:20.590
in having anybody see the work.

00:49:20.590 --> 00:49:22.321
Fortunately for me.

